NACL & Security Groups

In AWS, Security Groups are stateful firewalls applied at the instance level, while Network ACLs (NACLs) are stateless firewalls applied at the subnet level. Security Groups are easier to manage for individual EC2 instances, whereas NACLs provide broader subnet-wide control, including the ability to explicitly deny traffic.

🔑 Key Differences Between NACLs and Security Groups
Feature	Security Group (SG)	Network ACL (NACL)
Scope	Instance-level (attached to EC2)	Subnet-level (applies to all resources in subnet)
Statefulness	Stateful – return traffic is automatically allowed	Stateless – return traffic must be explicitly allowed
Rules	Only “allow” rules	Both “allow” and “deny” rules
Default Behavior	Inbound denied, outbound allowed	All inbound/outbound allowed by default
Granularity	Fine-grained control for individual instances	Broad subnet-wide control
Use Case	Protect specific EC2 instances	Block/allow traffic across entire subnet
Complexity	Easier to configure and maintain	More complex, requires explicit ephemeral port handling
