📌 When to Use Security Groups
Controlling access to specific EC2 instances.

Allowing traffic for applications (e.g., HTTP/HTTPS, SSH).

Simplifying management with stateful rules (no need to configure return traffic).

Applying multiple SGs to one instance for flexible configurations.

📌 When to Use NACLs
Enforcing subnet-wide rules across all resources.

Blocking specific IPs or ranges (since SGs don’t support deny rules).

Adding an extra layer of defense beyond Security Groups.

Meeting compliance requirements that demand explicit inbound/outbound restrictions.

✅ Best Practice
AWS recommends using both together for a layered security model:

Security Groups: First line of defense at the instance level.

NACLs: Second line of defense at the subnet level, especially for deny rules and broader control.

Bottom Line:  
Use Security Groups for everyday instance-level access control, and add NACLs when you need subnet-wide enforcement, deny rules, or layered protection. Together, they provide a robust defense-in-depth strategy in AWS networking.
